Make VP8's active map actually cheap: exit before the mode picker, and move the
skipped macroblocks' pixel copy out of the per-macroblock loop.

libvpx honours VP8E_SET_ACTIVEMAP already, but the check lives deep inside
evaluate_inter_mode(), which vp8_pick_inter_mode() only reaches after running
get_predictor_pointers(), the dot-artifact check (SAD over Y and both chroma
planes), the skin-map lookup, get_reference_search_order(),
vp8_find_near_mvs_bias() and part of the mode loop -- all for a macroblock the
caller has already said is not to be coded. And once past that, each excluded
macroblock still moved 384 bytes of reference pixels into the reconstruction
buffer one block at a time.

On hardware where VP8 is the bottleneck that is the frame rate. A remote desktop
excludes almost every macroblock almost every frame: at 1280x1024 a moving
pointer marks 6 to 20 of 5120 active, and stock libvpx still spends 450-600 ms
on the other 5100.

Two changes:

1. Exit at the top of vp8cx_encode_inter_macroblock(). The answer for an
   excluded macroblock is a definition rather than a search result -- nothing
   moved, so code it as ZEROMV against LAST and skip it. That is where the
   existing late exit lands anyway (it sets x->skip, the mode loop takes its
   `|| x->skip` branch and breaks), so this changes when the decision is made,
   not what it is. Placed here rather than inside the picker so it covers the RD
   path too.

2. Seed the whole reconstruction buffer from the reference once per frame, in
   init_encode_frame_mb_context(), when an active map is in force -- and drop
   the per-macroblock copy that becomes redundant. One contiguous copy of the
   same bytes has the locality and none of the per-call overhead of 5120 small
   scattered ones. Skipped on key frames, which code everything anyway.

Measured on IRIX 6.5.22m / R5000 under IRIS, the cost of a frame whose active
map selects *nothing at all* -- the floor under every frame however little
changed. Three interleaved rounds for (1), because a first attempt that ran
stock-then-patched measured the host's load average instead:

                stock          (1) only       (1) + (2)
   320x256       51/50/51 ms   39/34/37 ms    35 ms
   640x512      166/172/191 ms 95/91/94 ms    77 ms      (2.2x)
  1280x1024     753/708/712 ms 309/348/340 ms 250 ms     (2.9x)

The saving in (2) was priced first with a deliberately-wrong build that simply
omitted the per-macroblock copy and produced garbage in the untouched regions;
that measured the same 395 -> 250 ms at 1280x1024, which is what said the copy
was worth engineering around rather than guessing at.

Verified on the target, not just built: a 100-second session against a moving
desktop, every frame decoded by the peer, and the decoded picture compared with
a fresh capture of the same screen -- mean absolute difference 2.40 of 255, with
every large difference inside the one region (an xclock) that was still moving.
A wrongly-skipped or wrongly-copied macroblock would show up there as a block of
large differences and nowhere else.

--- a/vp8/encoder/encodeframe.c
+++ b/vp8/encoder/encodeframe.c
@@ -32,6 +32,8 @@
 #include <stdio.h>
 #include <limits.h>
 #include "vp8/common/invtrans.h"
+#include "vpx_scale/yv12config.h"
+#include "./vpx_scale_rtcd.h"
 #include "vpx_ports/vpx_timer.h"
 #if CONFIG_REALTIME_ONLY & CONFIG_ONTHEFLY_BITPACKING
 #include "bitstream.h"
@@ -595,6 +597,28 @@
   xd->pre = cm->yv12_fb[cm->lst_fb_idx];
   xd->dst = cm->yv12_fb[cm->new_fb_idx];
 
+  /* When an active map is in force, seed the whole reconstruction buffer from
+   * the reference in one go.
+   *
+   * Every macroblock the map excludes is coded as ZEROMV against LAST and
+   * skipped, and the only thing that still had to happen for it was moving 384
+   * bytes of reference pixels into the reconstruction buffer so the frame is
+   * complete. Doing that per macroblock means thousands of small scattered
+   * copies -- 5120 of them on a 1280x1024 frame -- where one contiguous copy of
+   * the same bytes has the locality and none of the per-call overhead.
+   *
+   * Priced on IRIX 6.5 / R5000 by building a deliberately-wrong encoder that
+   * simply omitted the per-macroblock copy: the cost of a frame whose map
+   * selects nothing fell from 395 ms to 250 ms at 1280x1024 and from 113 ms to
+   * 77 ms at 640x512. This recovers that saving without the wrong picture.
+   *
+   * Not on key frames: they code every macroblock by definition, so the copy
+   * would be overwritten in full and there is no reference to take it from.
+   */
+  if (cpi->active_map_enabled && cm->frame_type != KEY_FRAME) {
+    vp8_yv12_copy_frame(&cm->yv12_fb[cm->lst_fb_idx], &cm->yv12_fb[cm->new_fb_idx]);
+  }
+
   /* set up frame for intra coded blocks */
   vp8_setup_intra_recon(&cm->yv12_fb[cm->new_fb_idx]);
 
@@ -1122,6 +1146,61 @@
 
   x->skip = 0;
 
+  /* A macroblock the caller has excluded needs no search at all.
+   *
+   * libvpx honours the active map already, but only inside
+   * evaluate_inter_mode() -- by which point vp8_pick_inter_mode() has run
+   * get_predictor_pointers(), the dot-artifact check (SAD over Y and both
+   * chroma planes), the skin-map lookup, get_reference_search_order(),
+   * vp8_find_near_mvs_bias() and part of the mode loop, all to decide
+   * something the caller stated.
+   *
+   * On hardware where VP8 is the bottleneck that prologue is the frame rate.
+   * On IRIX 6.5 / R5000 at 640x512, a frame whose map selects *nothing* still
+   * costs 173 ms -- 0.135 ms per excluded macroblock, 1280 times -- and that is
+   * the floor under every frame however little changed. On a remote desktop,
+   * almost nothing changes.
+   *
+   * The answer here is a definition rather than a search result: nothing moved,
+   * so code it as ZEROMV against LAST and skip it. That is where the existing
+   * late exit lands anyway (it sets x->skip, the mode loop takes its `||
+   * x->skip` branch and breaks), so this changes when the decision is made and
+   * not what it is. Everything below -- the inter predictors, mb_skip_coeff,
+   * vp8_fix_contexts -- then runs as it does for any other skipped macroblock.
+   */
+  if (cpi->active_map_enabled && x->active_ptr[0] == 0) {
+    MB_MODE_INFO *mbmi = &xd->mode_info_context->mbmi;
+    mbmi->mode = ZEROMV;
+    mbmi->ref_frame = LAST_FRAME;
+    mbmi->mv.as_int = 0;
+    mbmi->uv_mode = DC_PRED;
+    mbmi->partitioning = 0;
+    mbmi->need_to_clamp_mvs = 0;
+    x->skip = 1;
+    x->mbs_tested_so_far++;
+    x->count_mb_ref_frame_usage[LAST_FRAME]++;
+
+    /* The reconstruction buffer already holds this block's reference pixels:
+     * init_encode_frame_mb_context() copied the whole reference in when the
+     * active map was enabled, precisely so this loop does not have to do it
+     * 5120 times. `pre` is still pointed at the reference because the entropy
+     * and mode bookkeeping below reads it. */
+    xd->pre.y_buffer =
+        cpi->common.yv12_fb[cpi->common.lst_fb_idx].y_buffer + recon_yoffset;
+    xd->pre.u_buffer =
+        cpi->common.yv12_fb[cpi->common.lst_fb_idx].u_buffer + recon_uvoffset;
+    xd->pre.v_buffer =
+        cpi->common.yv12_fb[cpi->common.lst_fb_idx].v_buffer + recon_uvoffset;
+    mbmi->mb_skip_coeff = 1;
+    if (cpi->common.mb_no_coeff_skip) {
+      x->skip_true_count++;
+      vp8_fix_contexts(xd);
+    } else {
+      vp8_stuff_mb(cpi, x, t);
+    }
+    return 0;
+  }
+
   if (xd->segmentation_enabled) {
     x->encode_breakout =
         cpi->segment_encode_breakout[xd->mode_info_context->mbmi.segment_id];
